@Composable

fun CcTheme(mode: ThemeMode, content: @Composable () -> Unit) {

    val dark = when (mode) { ThemeMode.Dark -> true; ThemeMode.Light -> false; ThemeMode.System -> isSystemInDarkTheme() }

    CompositionLocalProvider(LocalCcColors provides if (dark) DarkCcColors else LightCcColors) { MaterialTheme(content = co

ntent) }

}

Syntax colours in the dark theme use brighter versions: keyword #C792EA, type #4EC9B0, string
#CE9178, function #DCDCAA, number #B5CEA8, comment #6A9955, variable #9CDCFE, plain
#DBDEE1.

PART 1. HOW THE APP WORKS (read
before anything else)



MOCKUPS ARE THE LAW. Build exactly what the mockup pictures and the numbers in this document
show: the same spacing, sizes, icons, colours, corners, order of items and texts. Do not invent
anything that is not described here. Do not add, remove, move, resize, recolour or rename anything
because it seems better. Do not use a library default look (Material default colours, shapes, icon sets,
fonts, elevations) where this document gives a value. If something is missing or two things seem to
disagree, keep the existing behaviour of the app, copy the closest mockup, and ask. Before you call a
screen finished, put your screenshot next to the mockup picture and compare spacing, icons and text
one by one. Everything in the pictures that is a name, number or list is an example and must come
from real data.

This part explains the structure and the behaviour in plain words. Part 2 gives the Kotlin Jetpack
Compose code for every piece. Part 3 is the visual spec (numbers, colours, components, screens).
When the words and the code seem to disagree, the code in Part 2 is the reference.

1. The four layers (what is "full screen" and what is "half
screen")

Your developer is confused because the app has four different kinds of screen. Each has its own
rules.

