(the "two behind the editor card editor card slides right anywhere on card, swipe left, or
panels") and stays visible on the the editor (no Back

right edge button)

3. Full Clone, Source control, History, The whole phone, Tap an action Back button in the
screens Diff, Terminal session, Run, including the sidebar in a panel or a header, or system

Preview, Settings and its pages, and rail row in Settings Back. One step
Project settings, Welcome, Start back
project

4. Sheets, Project switcher, file menu, new Bottom part of the Tap on a button Back, tap outside,
menus, command, confirm dialogs screen or a floating box or long-press swipe down
dialogs over whatever is below

The two panels are NOT a minimised app. They are one thing: the sidebar. It is made of two parts
side by side:

Rail: 66 dp wide. Only icons. The tool list (Files, Search, GitHub, Terminal, Logcat, Problems) and
Settings at the bottom. Project avatar on top.
Panel: the rest of the 300 dp (234 dp). The panel is a rounded card: its top-left AND top-right
corners are rounded, radius 26 (the right corner is where the editor card starts). It shows the
content of the selected tool: file tree for Files, results for Search, and so on.

The editor card is on top of the sidebar. When the user swipes, the card moves to the right and the
sidebar is revealed under it. The card is still showing (a strip of the card on the right edge, with
rounded left corners, radius 26), slightly smaller and rounded, and it is dimmed. Tapping it closes the
sidebar. The sidebar is not a separate screen and it does not have its own header.



2. The state machine (the logic of the whole app)

Keep ONE source of truth for navigation. Do not let each screen decide for itself.

AppState

  project: Project?                 // the open project (real, from the database)

  sidebarOpen: Boolean              // layer 2

  sidebarTool: Tool                 // Files, Search, GitHub, Terminal, Logcat, Problems

  stack: List<Route>                // layer 3, empty when we are on the editor

  sheet: Sheet?                     // layer 4 (only one at a time)

  openFiles: List<OpenFile>         // editor tabs

  activeFile: OpenFile?

Rules, in the order the system Back button must handle them (the first one that applies wins):

1. If a sheet, menu or dialog is open: close it. Stop.
2. If the stack has a full screen: pop one route. Stop.
3. If the sidebar is open: close it. Stop.
4. If we are on the editor and the last Back was less than 2 seconds ago: finish the app.
5. Otherwise show the toast "Press back again to exit" and remember the time.

Every full screen header has a back button that calls exactly the same function as the system Back (on
Back()), so they can never disagree.

