Navigation and back rules

Layout

The editor is the root screen. It fills the full width. There is no permanent sidebar and no bottom bar.

Action Result

Swipe right anywhere on the editor (mostly The editor card slides right 300 dp and keeps a 60 dp strip visible.
horizontal, and the code cannot scroll Its left corners are rounded, radius 26. The tool rail (66) and the side
further left). A swipe from the left 40 dp panel appear behind it. Follows the finger, settles open past 40% or
always works on a fast fling

Tap the dimmed editor card, or swipe left Sidebar closes

Tap a rail tool The panel changes to that tool. The sidebar stays open



Action Result

Tap a file in the Files panel The file opens in the editor, the sidebar closes

Tap the project avatar Project switcher sheet

Tap a tool's action button (for example A full screen opens over the sidebar, with a back button
Clone)

There is no menu button that opens the sidebar. Swipe is the only way, and it works from anywhere
on the editor.

Back rules

1. Every screen that is not the editor has the back button as the first item in the header.
2. The back button and the system Back do exactly the same: go back one step, to the screen the
user came from.

3. A sheet, menu or dialog closes first, before any screen goes back.
4. When the sidebar is open, Back closes the sidebar.
5. At the editor with the sidebar closed, the first Back shows the toast "Press back again to exit"
for 2 seconds. A second Back inside that time leaves the app. Do not exit on the first press.

6. Unsaved text in a form is kept when going back and forth. Ask before discarding only when the
user has typed something that cannot be rebuilt.

7. Keep the back stack as a real stack. Opening Settings then Developer then back returns to
Settings, not to the editor.

Header shows where you came from

Every nested header shows two texts: the origin screen in bold (the screen the user came from) and
the current screen in muted text after it. The origin is whatever screen opened this one, so build it
from the real back stack and do not hard-code a table. Typical results:

Current screen Origin shown in bold Muted text

Clone a repository GitHub Clone a repository

Source control GitHub Source control

History Source control History

A commit History the short hash of that commit

Shell Terminal the shell name

Preview Run Preview

Console (preview) Preview Console

Project settings the project name Project settings

Any settings page Settings the page name

Developer Settings Developer

If the origin name is long, it is cut with an ellipsis before the muted text is cut. If there is no origin (the
screen was opened from outside), show only the current name in bold.

Project switcher



Bottom sheet. Lists the user's real projects (name, tile in the project colour, letter, last-opened time).
The current one is marked with a check. Buttons: New project, Open folder, Clone a repository. When
there are no projects the list is replaced by the empty state described in the States section.

